Lets the core download Google's Widevine module, on Linux, once the user said yes to it in the
protected-media note (#236), and remove it from Settings. The module is never bundled, as Google's
licence forbids it.

The component updater checks only the three security components
(ardia/chromium-component-updater-security-only.patch). This lets it check Widevine's too, when
g_browser_core_component_allowed says the user allowed it (//browser_core/hooks.h). Its update URL
is already Google's own there, written in full after domain substitution, so nothing else is
un-substituted, and every other component stays out. The core downloads it at the user's yes
through the on-demand updater, which names the classes it lets call it, so the core's is added.
Removing it unregisters the component, which deletes it, and a later yes registers it again:
RegisterWidevineCdmComponent takes a callback for once it's registered. Chrome's other callers
pass none and work as before.

